1785268348668664	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2BA		item.comment	bn-1gn1	{"body":"DECLINED per acceptance criterion (6): production unchanged. Implementation and tests are complete and green, but acceptance (5) could not be demonstrated on this host — the measurement, not the change, is what failed.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION (retained in workspace bn-1gn1, NOT merged):\n- Diagnosis: OWNER_RING_BYTES (64 MiB) bounded only QUEUED intents. PreparedBatch::encode materializes the whole framed copy on the producer task and the permit was acquired AFTER it, so N concurrent producers could each hold a fully built batch (up to MAX_BATCH_LEN) entirely off the books.\n- Fix: reserve a conservative build peak against the SAME ring before preparing, then reconcile down to the exact admission cost. Satisfies (1): no new queue, no priority scheduling, no size-class reordering — it is the ring every append already passes through, acquired a few statements earlier.\n- (2) prepare_build_peak() counts PreparedBatch::type_id_offsets, ::payload_ranges, the subframes vector, type_slots, the borrowed path name-interning map, and full type-name bytes.\n- (3)/(4) exact release on both public paths and on mid-flight cancellation, tested.\n- Defect found and fixed during implementation: the first cut had reconcile acquire a shortfall WHILE HOLDING the reservation (hold-and-wait). With the ring near capacity two concurrent producers would deadlock the whole append path. Now debug_assert + release-then-reacquire, with type-name bytes counted so the branch is unreachable.\n- Tests: 8/8 append_gate_tests. prepare_build_peak_never_under_estimates_prepared_cost pins the over-estimate invariant across 1/10/100/1000 frames x payload sizes x chain on/off x 200-char names.\n\nBOUND DEMONSTRATED: concurrent high-water 4,273,951 B against the 67,108,864 B ring. Note trunk reads 4,228,822 B on the same gauge but that number EXCLUDES construction memory (precisely the bug), so the near-equality means construction memory is small in this workload, not that trunk was bounded.\n\nWHY (5) IS UNRESOLVED — the harness is noise-dominated here:\n- NULL CONTROL: process/1/uniform is a 4,212-byte batch (72+16+28+4096) vs PREPARE_MIN_ENCODED_BYTES=16,384, so can_prepare is false and this change is a literal no-op on that cell. It measured 94.5% and FAILED the >=95% bar. A no-op path cannot regress; the bar is inside the noise.\n- Same cell, opposite verdicts: group/100/uniform gave 75.3% in the ABBA matrix and 117.2% in an ABBA focused run minutes earlier (1000 batches instead of 200).\n- Single-build spread on Group cells is ~2x run-to-run (15,485-30,259 ev/s).\n- Every run today fired \"mess DEGRADED: fdatasync latency crossed 50 ms\" warnings; mess_log.durable mean_fsync swung 2484->3319 us between two full mess-bench runs.\n\nRIG BUG FOUND AND CORRECTED MID-EFFORT: the first A/B script ran trunk first and bn1gn1 second in every round, so the second build always started while the first run writeback was draining — a systematic penalty landing hardest on the fsync-bound Group cells. Re-run ABBA (T,B,B,T per pair) with sync+3s settle. That flipped group/100/uniform from a stable-looking 77.0%/77.1% \"regression\" to 117.2%. Any future attempt MUST use ABBA ordering; trunk-first-always fabricates Group regressions.\n\nTO RETRY: quiet host (no browsers/Discord; this box ran at load ~2.3), and raise per-cell sample count well above the 200-batch matrix sizing — the focused run at 1000 batches was materially steadier. Bench harness lives at crates/mess-store/benches/prepared_bound_matrix.rs in the workspace (MESS_BENCH_ONLY / MESS_BENCH_BATCHES env filters); scripts under the session scratchpad."}	blake3:aX9AxmhSNfB33RJxxn0JfinDAfoEv-0LJzvZ3wEiOHc
1785268352227685	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2RA		item.move	bn-1gn1	{"state":"open","unstart":true}	blake3:EyoECNupm4gAkBNu1b7Gz-_pvxvlNRmU20t9pTKL_8M
1785268352255828	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2hA		item.assign	bn-1gn1	{"action":"unassign","agent":"mess-dev"}	blake3:yKjLJvauGYYSGZ2-RACwuk920tiWAeavFcAZX3Kplmo
1785274314323045	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2xA		item.comment	bn-o5b	{"body":"FRAMING CORRECTION (2026-07-28, Bob): the roadmap is a set of HYPOTHESES to try and keep or drop, not a certification program.\n\nPhase 4 drifted into treating per-bone \"Acceptance Criteria\" blocks as hard gates — matched batch-stratified matrices, >=1.25x/<=0.75x thresholds, ADMIT/DECLINE verdicts, committed raw evidence. bn-1gn1 showed the cost: crates/mess-bench full run answered the question in ~2 min (all 11 floor-gated metrics PASS on both trunk and the change), then a bespoke 17-cell acceptance matrix was built to satisfy the bone text, was too noisy to resolve its own 95%/110% bar, and produced a DECLINE on a change that was fine. A null-control cell (process/1/uniform, 4,212 bytes vs the 16,384-byte preparation threshold, so the change was a literal no-op there) measured 94.5% against a >=95% bar — proof the bar sat inside the noise.\n\nTHE LOOP FROM HERE:\n1. Implement the hypothesis.\n2. cargo build -p mess-bench --release; MESS_BENCH_DIR=$HOME/.cache/mess-bench ./target/release/mess-bench run --mode full --out <ledger> --floors crates/mess-bench/floors.json\n3. Better or neutral -> keep. Clearly worse -> drop it or investigate why.\n4. Next hypothesis.\n\nfloors.json with its -10% per-metric tolerance IS the ratchet, and it is coarse enough to survive this host's noise — which is exactly why it works where a 5% gate did not. Ordinary correctness still gates normally: just test + just fmt-check + seal review.\n\nWHAT THIS SUPERSEDES: the constraint above reading \"Use matched, batch-stratified composed benchmarks and committed raw evidence; isolated microbench wins do not waive end-to-end gates\", and the Acceptance Criteria measurement clauses in child bones. Those are notes from a more paranoid moment, not contracts. Treat them as context.\n\nWHEN HEAVY MEASUREMENT IS STILL WARRANTED: when a real product decision actually hinges on the number (default-on/off, deleting a subsystem, a compatibility policy) AND the expected effect is large enough to resolve above noise. That is rare and deliberate, not the default. If a heavy comparison is ever run again: ABBA ordering (trunk-first-always fabricates Group regressions via draining writeback), >=1000 batches per cell, a null-control cell that must read ~100%, and a quiet host."}	blake3:JGX6ehWdqB2wF0y69crMJIQPNdH43iXWp9OrksCpjqs
1785274322562539	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx3BA		item.comment	bn-3ef	{"body":"Framing: see the FRAMING CORRECTION comment on parent bn-o5b (2026-07-28). This phase is a set of hypotheses, not a certification program. Default gate for each child change is `mess-bench run --mode full --floors crates/mess-bench/floors.json` (~2 min, -10% per-metric ratchet) plus the ordinary `just test` / `just fmt-check` / seal review. Better or neutral -> keep; clearly worse -> drop or investigate. The batch-stratified matrices and ADMIT/DECLINE thresholds written into child Acceptance Criteria are context, not contracts."}	blake3:TyUKxiXGjInjnkrIoRoswyT0dO0EVubGOGw2vK8UeEM
