1785276063613934	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-RA		item.comment	bn-1gn1	{"body":"UN-DECLINED and merged to trunk @ 70d21095, per the bn-o5b FRAMING CORRECTION. The earlier DECLINE was faithful to this bone's written Acceptance Criteria (5) and wrong in substance: this is a memory-safety bound whose perf criterion was only \"do not make things worse\", and mess-bench full passed all 11 floor-gated metrics on both trunk and the change. The bespoke 17-cell acceptance matrix that produced the DECLINE could not resolve its own 95%/110% bar — its null-control cell, a code path this change does not touch, measured 94.5%.\n\nMerged WITHOUT the paired-matrix bench harness (crates/mess-store/benches/prepared_bound_matrix.rs); that was apparatus, not product. Only the engine bound and its tests landed.\n\nVerification: just test 984 tests, 983 passed. Sole failure was social::seed_profile seed_profile_smoke, which passes 3/3 isolated (1.18s) and fails only under full-suite load (11.6s) — see bn-squ3. mess-bench full: all 11 floors PASS."}	blake3:zl3_JvT9annUsyVQkJgWDPg61EK9But-cK_ASgCb-jk
1785276063631268	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-hA		item.move	bn-1gn1	{"state":"done"}	blake3:grIugVFef9-O9aTTwFUBi6mAI8OzEgv-kiHfZ57QLrw
1785276074456117	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-xA		item.create	bn-2cye	{"description":"examples/social/tests/seed_profile.rs:716 asserts that new-stream append p50 EXCEEDS warm append p50 (new streams pay a registry name-persist fsync, bn-34o). Under full-suite load on 2026-07-28 it measured new=18.097us vs warm=19.118us and failed — a 1us, ~5% gap that landed on the wrong side.\n\nPasses 3/3 in isolation (1.18s per run); fails only under 'just test' parallel load (11.6s for the same test). Same class of problem as bn-1av (soak crash-cycle count) and as the bn-1gn1 acceptance matrix: a hard perf assertion whose margin is inside the host's noise floor.\n\nFix options: (a) require a multiplicative margin rather than strict ordering (e.g. new >= 1.5x warm), (b) mark it #[ignore] and run it only on a quiet host, or (c) assert the fsync COUNT difference rather than wall-clock p50 — the underlying claim is 'new streams pay an extra fsync', which is directly countable and not timing-dependent. (c) is the real fix: it tests the mechanism instead of its performance shadow.","kind":"bug","labels":["flaky","dx"],"title":"flaky: seed_profile_smoke asserts a microsecond-scale perf ordering with no noise margin"}	blake3:heh2Tgr7q40ds5lVfMEpkwAT37svn87QI0Sh2V3lmhg
1785276078286861	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_BA		item.comment	bn-1gn1	{"body":"Correction: the flake bone referenced above is bn-2cye, not bn-squ3 (bn-squ3 does not exist; bn-squ2 is the edict responder bug)."}	blake3:3Vc9WYld0WdvSwtojK_r7R7VbXfgFzQeuhtYkIoEMek
1785278901070003	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_RA		item.comment	bn-3ef	{"body":"Closing Phase 4. 12 of 13 children done; the sole remainder is bn-2l3n, the punted asterism rebaseline — shelved attestation work, not engineering (see bn-o5b FRAMING CORRECTION). The flat-owner kernel work this phase existed to finish is complete: owned/interned append API (bn-2yye), Process-only owned append behind a fresh gate (bn-22it), grouped publication (bn-3vj4), direct outcomes + reusable scratch (bn-21ew), byte-ring fairness and cancellation (bn-3eg0), coalesced pwritev (bn-1zv6/bn-1l4j), and bounded prepared construction memory (bn-1gn1, trunk 70d21095)."}	blake3:OGfe5EUwEeKhfUx-nnGb-0bEWFRf9xdQTPXVbgsZqe0
1785278901087041	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_hA		item.move	bn-3ef	{"state":"done"}	blake3:sZeVEhXFo3daNR1GJv3eUh6OHBqalz5feXMHAN-Ugwo
1785278946950490	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_xA		item.comment	bn-11mk	{"body":"DECLINE — engine-owned projection checkpoints. Decided 2026-07-28 (mess-dev, delegated by Bob).\n\nEVIDENCE (verified in code, not inferred from the bone text):\n- MetaStore::set_checkpoint / checkpoint / checkpoint_lag / dump_checkpoints live in crates/mess-index/src/meta/mod.rs:402,519,572,593. Every caller in the tree is a test: crates/mess-index/tests/meta.rs:90-92,252-260. Zero production callers.\n- The one real consumer already solved this itself and PROVED it correct. examples/social owns a discardable sidecar (store_backend::checkpoint_path, Projections::with_checkpoint) plus examples/social/src/rebuild.rs, a --rebuild check that byte-compares a checkpoint-resumed read model against a clean from-zero rebuild. It catches exactly the failures engine ownership would be sold as preventing: a stale checkpoint trusted too far, a suffix-replay off-by-one.\n- The backing store is scheduled for deletion anyway. bn-fj34 deletes mess-index::meta outright and explicitly states projection checkpoints do not block removal.\n\nRATIONALE: no user, no job, no adopted caller — the ADMIT branch could not satisfy its own first acceptance criterion (\"states the concrete user/job value, adopted caller\"). Application-owned discardable state is also the better default here: a projection is the application's model, its checkpoint is an optimization of the application's replay, and making the engine authoritative for it converts a discardable cache into a durability and corruption surface the engine must then defend forever. Building dense projection slots for it is precisely the speculative machinery bn-o5b now rejects.\n\nCONSEQUENCES:\n- bn-1lbf (dense latest-value slots) closed as conditional work that will not happen.\n- Dormant MetaStore checkpoint APIs need no separate removal bone: bn-fj34 already deletes mess-index::meta entirely.\n- The social sidecar stays explicitly discardable and documented as the recommended pattern.\n\nREVISIT IF: a real consumer appears that needs a checkpoint the application cannot own — e.g. an engine-internal projection required for recovery correctness, or a cross-application shared read model with no single owner. Neither exists today."}	blake3:Fb0T62uR_4l_Lq4KUaRzzpqJxjtj6Bshyx3Ci-2gYR8
1785278946967165	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExgBE		item.move	bn-11mk	{"state":"done"}	blake3:J1fnZ-n6l0tOTzfOCuf7mPHnotyjCURiV9iF5ndzcWw
1785278960943392	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExgRE		item.comment	bn-2ctq	{"body":"DECLINE (for v3) — exact batch idempotency / engine-level dedupe. Decided 2026-07-28 (mess-dev, delegated by Bob).\n\nEVIDENCE:\n- The public append API carries no idempotency key today: append_batch(stream, expected, records) / append_batch_owned(...). Nothing to preserve.\n- There is no production dedupe. The only \"dedupe\" in mess-store is an index-read concern, not idempotency: EngineOptions::dedupe_capacity (engine.rs:2334) feeds a derived cache that dedupes two-tier index entries by batch identity/first_version (engine.rs:3784,3800). Unrelated capability, same word.\n- Spike G admitted an exact epoch structure MECHANICALLY. It never established that the public semantics and ongoing resource cost are worth shipping — which is what this bone exists to decide.\n\nRATIONALE: callers already have a correct retry story that costs the engine nothing. Optimistic concurrency (expected: Version) makes a duplicate retry observable — the retry conflicts, the caller re-reads and decides. That is caller-level idempotency with zero permanent engine surface. Engine-level exact dedupe is the opposite: a retention frontier that must outlive segment deletion, collision exactness under adversarial keys, crash atomicity of key+event as one unit, namespace abuse limits, and memory/disk caps — a large permanent correctness and security surface, adopted for no identified consumer. ADR 0002 also notes two ordered v3 batches are not atomic enough, so a v3 bridge would be genuinely hard, not just tedious.\n\nCONSEQUENCES:\n- bn-3dp1 (port exact active/frozen dedupe epochs into mess-index) and bn-1sh8 (integrate exact dedupe through append/recovery/observability) closed as conditional work.\n- No dormant production API or table is left behind: there is none to remove.\n\nNOT A PERMANENT NO. v4 puts one DedupeKey per capsule, so admitting v4 re-opens this by construction — at which point the semantics are set by the format rather than invented here. This decision is scoped to v3 and should be revisited as part of the v4 admission decision (bn-1ojm, bn-1zm0), which is itself still open and unprejudiced by this."}	blake3:-AgCxrSl-XKt_IxsNEc57cvWMk9OwBYcbkjS58gfS8o
