1785293650501937	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjxE		item.move	bn-34v8	{"state":"open","unstart":true}	blake3:lnyxT__xyBijqrnp4pUmxyb7YV8H_80ddCndF1IVNMk
1785293664570159	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkBE		item.create	bn-1dlb	{"description":"mess-bench's eight reference workloads contain no concurrent readers — verified: no 'spawn' and no '.head(' anywhere in crates/mess-bench/src/workloads/. Every engine metric is a single-writer loop. So the entire Phase 5 premise (dense heads beat the shared Arc<Mutex<Book>>) is currently unmeasurable by the tool we've made the default gate.\n\nGOAL: add one permanent reference workload — N concurrent readers calling head()/stream_id_of() against an active writer — and measure trunk as it stands.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND PHASE 5:\n- If the Book mutex is not contended under realistic reader load, dense heads cannot pay: bn-34v8, bn-2wtl and bn-2ig9 all collapse and Phase 5 is nearly empty.\n- If it IS contended, this workload reports the split between head reads and name resolution. engine.rs:4405 head(name) resolves name->id via book.registry under the SAME lock, and the other Book readers (stream_id_of:3709, event_type_id_of:3717, record decode:3816) are ALL name resolution. If the contention is mostly name->id, dense heads target the wrong half and the real bone (currently unwritten) is getting name resolution off that mutex.\n\nThis is a permanent addition to the reference set, not measurement apparatus: it covers a real usage pattern (concurrent readers against a live writer) that ships today and is completely unmeasured. It should earn a floors.json entry and run in CI.\n\nDeliberately NOT the ABBA/paired rig from bn-1gn1 — that was throwaway. This is one workload file plus a floors entry, judged by the ordinary -10% ratchet.","kind":"task","labels":["benchmark","mess-bench","asterism"],"title":"Add a concurrent-reader workload to mess-bench and measure Book mutex contention"}	blake3:LQVK9mY5SFhoVSJDT8bMApGw5lYi-IdKGYLH6MlS5Rw
1785293669469583	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkRE		item.move	bn-1dlb	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:BUH2t7rQzIGtwgKwtvNRJe_S7moVq5aEkx1lFANDpb0
1785293669486072	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkhE		item.assign	bn-1dlb	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-dev"}	blake3:1jpKxxj9uqolP9GkOfbjAY5LspuE9LWOcSnFtKAVQeY
1785294406506006	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkxE		item.comment	bn-1dlb	{"body":"Merged to trunk @ 2456a779. RESULT — the measurement answers Phase 5's premise, and the answer is not the one the roadmap assumed.\n\nTwo full runs on trunk 70d21095:\n  writer_retention   0.84 / 0.80    (8 concurrent readers cost the writer 16-20%)\n  head_reads_per_s   3,004,531 / 2,543,928\n  resolve_per_s      2,979,045 / 2,518,286\n\nFINDING 1 — the Book mutex IS contended. 8 readers take 16-20% off writer throughput. Real, reproducible, worth attacking.\n\nFINDING 2 — dense heads attack the wrong part of it. head(name) does name->id resolution AND the head-map read under one lock; stream_id_of(name) does the resolution ONLY. They come out within ~1% of each other in BOTH runs (0.85% and 1.0%). The head-map read is therefore ~1% of that critical section.\n\nThe sharper reading: the cost is the LOCK ACQUISITION, not what is under it. Both operations are dominated by serialising on the same mutex, so the marginal work inside barely registers. That is why dense heads cannot pay as scoped — head(name) still has to take the Book lock to resolve the name, so moving heads to a lock-free table removes ~1% of the work while keeping 100% of the serialisation.\n\nCONSEQUENCE for Phase 5: bn-34v8 (dense head pages), bn-2wtl (shadow + cutover) and bn-2ig9 (hardening) cannot recover the 16-20%. The real target is name->id resolution, or making the read path lock-free wholesale. Recommend closing all three as measured-not-worth-it and writing a new bone against name resolution.\n\nFloors: writer_loaded, head_reads_per_s and resolve_per_s gated at ~half measured (deliberately loose — new concurrent workload, uncharacterised variance). writer_retention and writer_solo intentionally ungated: the ratio is host-scheduling dependent, and solo is an internal reference. Revisit tolerances once several CI runs exist."}	blake3:upSzVDdiiyWB_gaj82ZcD9toI7HI7bmAy9x_xsnsJw0
1785294406528639	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlBE		item.move	bn-1dlb	{"state":"done"}	blake3:WWGDo3WxZyFHEmjFj8oDJs3C9SnlWCjm7zg1V4wMk7s
1785350211065406	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlRE		item.comment	bn-34v8	{"body":"CLOSED as measured-not-worth-it (2026-07-28), per bn-1dlb.\n\nThe concurrent-reader workload measured Phase 5's premise directly on trunk 70d21095, twice:\n  writer_retention  0.84 / 0.80    (8 readers cost the writer 16-20%)\n  head_reads_per_s  3,004,531 / 2,543,928\n  resolve_per_s     2,979,045 / 2,518,286\n\nThe Book mutex is genuinely contended — but head(name) and stream_id_of(name) land within ~1% of each other in both runs (0.85%, 1.0%). head() does name->id resolution AND the head-map read under one lock; stream_id_of does resolution only. So the head-map read is ~1% of that critical section: the cost is the LOCK ACQUISITION, not the work under it.\n\nDense heads therefore cannot recover the 16-20%. head(name) must still take the Book lock to resolve the name, so a lock-free head table removes ~1% of the work and keeps 100% of the serialisation.\n\nSpike A's component numbers (16.02 bytes/stream, 33-43M applies/s) are not disputed — they measured a structure whose cost is not where the composed engine's cost is. That is exactly the failure mode bn-1n7j's own \"synthetic component wins alone do not admit integration\" anticipated.\n\nNo production code was written for any of these three. Successor work targets the lock itself: see the new bone.\n\nREVISIT IF: the read path is later restructured so name resolution is off the Book mutex, at which point the head map could become the next thing in line and this measurement should be re-run rather than assumed."}	blake3:4GKQRysfS6gW3tOc9_7maMhvblDqRsv3vKqHKDtbgIw
1785350211085630	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlhE		item.move	bn-34v8	{"state":"done"}	blake3:JpRnxIIszOtI5U96kfpdk-_No96vtRrDyiswzrzv4qA
1785350211105490	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlxE		item.comment	bn-2wtl	{"body":"CLOSED as measured-not-worth-it (2026-07-28), per bn-1dlb.\n\nThe concurrent-reader workload measured Phase 5's premise directly on trunk 70d21095, twice:\n  writer_retention  0.84 / 0.80    (8 readers cost the writer 16-20%)\n  head_reads_per_s  3,004,531 / 2,543,928\n  resolve_per_s     2,979,045 / 2,518,286\n\nThe Book mutex is genuinely contended — but head(name) and stream_id_of(name) land within ~1% of each other in both runs (0.85%, 1.0%). head() does name->id resolution AND the head-map read under one lock; stream_id_of does resolution only. So the head-map read is ~1% of that critical section: the cost is the LOCK ACQUISITION, not the work under it.\n\nDense heads therefore cannot recover the 16-20%. head(name) must still take the Book lock to resolve the name, so a lock-free head table removes ~1% of the work and keeps 100% of the serialisation.\n\nSpike A's component numbers (16.02 bytes/stream, 33-43M applies/s) are not disputed — they measured a structure whose cost is not where the composed engine's cost is. That is exactly the failure mode bn-1n7j's own \"synthetic component wins alone do not admit integration\" anticipated.\n\nNo production code was written for any of these three. Successor work targets the lock itself: see the new bone.\n\nREVISIT IF: the read path is later restructured so name resolution is off the Book mutex, at which point the head map could become the next thing in line and this measurement should be re-run rather than assumed."}	blake3:_l2f3MBPe7c5WXFSxV-ArDmFrQX7Ngx3cjt35_q3ehM
