1785350246041856	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnBE		item.create	bn-18ab	{"description":"Successor to bn-34v8/bn-2wtl/bn-2ig9, which were closed as measured-not-worth-it. Grounded in bn-1dlb's measurement rather than a spike.\n\nMEASUREMENT (trunk 70d21095, two full mess-bench runs):\n  writer_retention  0.84 / 0.80    -- 8 concurrent readers cost the writer 16-20%\n  head_reads_per_s  3,004,531 / 2,543,928\n  resolve_per_s     2,979,045 / 2,518,286\n\nhead(name) does name->id resolution AND the head-map read under one lock; stream_id_of(name) does resolution only. They land within ~1% of each other in both runs. So the cost is the LOCK ACQUISITION, not the work under it — which is why replacing the head map (bn-34v8) could not have paid.\n\nGOAL: make the common read path not serialise on Arc<Mutex<Book>> at all. Target the 16-20% writer retention loss and the reader rates, measured by engine.reader_contention.* in mess-bench.\n\nWHAT IS UNDER THE LOCK (crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:213 Book): registry (RegistryState), stream_arcs, type_arcs, heads, registry_next_version, registry_lost. Readers touching it: head (4405), stream_id_of (3709), event_type_id_of (3717), record decode (3816). All engine clones share ONE Arc<Mutex<Book>> (191).\n\nRegistryState (registry/state.rs:142) is NameTable + HashMap fields; stream_id (190) is a pure lookup. Registrations are rare (new streams/types only); reads are hot. That asymmetry is the opening.\n\nDIRECTIONS TO EVALUATE, cheapest first — do NOT build all of these:\n1. arc-swap / RCU: readers clone an Arc<RegistryState> snapshot with no lock; the owner publishes a new one on registration. Rare writes, wait-free reads. Probably the best fit.\n2. RwLock instead of Mutex: readers share. Cheapest possible change; may be enough, and worth measuring FIRST precisely because it is nearly free to try.\n3. Split the lock: separate registry state from heads/allocator so the two do not contend even when both are needed.\n\nMETHOD (per bn-o5b FRAMING CORRECTION): try the cheapest first, measure with mess-bench full, keep it if writer_retention and the reader rates improve, drop it otherwise. One hypothesis at a time. Do not build the elaborate version before the simple one has been measured — that is the mistake bn-34v8 was rescoped to avoid.\n\nCAUTION: mess-bench's reader-contention workload uses 8 readers on an 8-worker runtime, which is a favourable shape for showing contention. Confirm any win is not just a scheduling artefact by checking that reader rates rise WITHOUT writer throughput falling.","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","engine","perf","roadmap","state-kernel"],"title":"Get the engine read path off the shared Book mutex"}	blake3:skCmiaWWs6faPG6OHDUsjEhcdRLeecPhy80AqTj8IUE
1785350258583815	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnRE		item.comment	bn-o5b	{"body":"Phase 5 (bn-1n7j) auto-completed 2026-07-28. Outcome summary, since it is not what the roadmap projected:\n\n- bn-11mk DECLINE: engine-owned projection checkpoints have zero production callers; the one real consumer (examples/social) already owns a discardable sidecar with a byte-compare rebuild proof. Closed bn-1lbf.\n- bn-2ctq DECLINE (v3 only): no idempotency key exists in the public API, and optimistic concurrency already gives callers a correct retry story. Closed bn-3dp1, bn-1sh8. Reopens by construction if v4 is admitted (one DedupeKey per capsule).\n- bn-34v8 / bn-2wtl / bn-2ig9 CLOSED as measured-not-worth-it: bn-1dlb measured the premise and the Book mutex cost turned out to be the LOCK ACQUISITION, not the head map (head() and stream_id_of() within ~1% across two runs).\n\nNet: 7 bones, 0 production components built, 1 permanent bench workload added, and the actual bottleneck located. Successor bn-18ab targets the lock itself.\n\nThis is the framing working as intended — the phase resolved by measuring cheaply rather than by building the planned machinery and certifying it afterwards. Worth noting the same treatment likely applies to Phase 6 (bn-34as, microblocks replacing active stream indexes): it is a structural-replacement hypothesis of exactly the same shape as dense heads, and should get a cheap measurement of the composed cost BEFORE any component port (bn-1fsu)."}	blake3:ltCrEn2APkdev40MzHLpzt4HTsSw0rK7zP4VfTcdVJc
1785364848461343	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnhE		item.move	bn-18ab	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:vEnkbcBgrF6QF48HLHnK5F_SiEo94sB18xIQvuOsrgY
1785364848464744	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnxE		item.assign	bn-18ab	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-dev"}	blake3:F7e287PHFXsv87lGO8DrQslVIIMXM-nLGgQUXg3y3XY
1785375109088186	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoBE		item.comment	bn-18ab	{"body":"PROGRESS + a self-inflicted measurement error, recorded so it is not repeated.\n\nRESULT SO FAR — direction 2 (RwLock instead of Mutex) works. Two full mess-bench runs:\n                     trunk (Mutex)      RwLock\n  writer_retention   0.84 / 0.80        0.95 / 0.98\n  writer_loaded      20.77M / 20.83M    23.13M / 22.46M\n  head_reads_per_s   3.00M / 2.54M      3.72M / 3.11M\n  resolve_per_s      2.98M / 2.52M      3.83M / 3.13M\nAll 14 floors PASS both runs. Reader rates rose WITHOUT writer throughput falling — the specific check this bone required against a scheduling artefact. The 16-20% contention tax is now 2-5%, so directions 1 (arc-swap/RCU) and 3 (split the lock) are unnecessary: do not build them.\n\nChange is Mutex -> RwLock plus 12 call sites (3 write, 9 read). One site had .lock() split across lines and was missed by grep — caught by the compiler.\n\nNEW HAZARD DOCUMENTED IN CODE: std::sync::RwLock does not guarantee read recursion, so a second read guard taken while holding one can deadlock against a queued writer. Unlike a Mutex self-deadlock this is load-dependent and would not show in a quiet test run. All 12 sites audited: every one is a single lookup in its own scope, and the two owner paths that call further engine code (commit_plans, plan_domain) take their guard in a block or if-condition that drops first. Constraint written onto the Book doc comment.\n\nMY ERROR — orphaned load generators. To test whether the cancellation flake was load-dependent I ran `for j in $(seq 1 16); do (while :; do :; done) & done` twice. `jobs -p` did not capture those PIDs in a non-interactive shell, so `kill $STRESS` silently did nothing and 32 busy-loop shells kept running at 100% until Bob killed them manually. I then started four full test suites on top of them. Consequences: (a) the \"0/15 failures under load\" results from those runs are meaningless and are discarded; (b) paired-trunk-1 / paired-rwlock-1 ran under that phantom load — still fair as a PAIR since both saw it, but not clean. Never simulate CPU load with unbounded background shells; use stress-ng or taskset-bounded workers with a hard timeout, and verify the cleanup actually killed them.\n\nCANCELLATION FLAKE — attribution so far (6 full suites):\n  trunk:  1 failure  (dropped_append_future_does_not_gap_the_position_sequence)\n  RwLock: 2 failures (cancel_then_same_stream_retry_never_double_writes_version; cancellation_after_owned_transfer_still_publishes)\nThree DIFFERENT tests in the same family, on BOTH builds, roughly 1 per 1000 tests. All share one mechanism: poll_once_then_drop polls once with a noop waker and asserts Pending, which is racy regardless of lock type. bn-31 already documented this family at 3 occurrences before this change. Evidence points at the tests, not the lock — but trunk n=1 is thin, so paired runs on a now-quiet machine are finishing before any merge."}	blake3:DViMmQvGWHXWORlsTxviwrCleCTIRJEikbpxl8Vx27Q
1785377501753813	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoRE		item.comment	bn-18ab	{"body":"MERGED to trunk @ 4f9a3dd7. Direction 2 (RwLock) was sufficient; directions 1 (arc-swap/RCU) and 3 (split the lock) are NOT needed and should not be built — the 16-20% tax is now 2-5%.\n\nCancellation-flake attribution, resolved. Four paired full suites on a quiet machine:\n  pair 1 (contaminated by my orphaned load generators): trunk 1 FAIL, RwLock 984/984\n  pair 2 (clean):                                       trunk 984/984, RwLock 984/984\nThe only failure in the paired comparison was on TRUNK. Across all six full suites today: trunk 1 failure / 2 runs, RwLock 2 / 4 — indistinguishable, and three DIFFERENT tests. Confirmed pre-existing family flake, not a regression from this change.\n\nFollow-up worth doing (not blocking): the cancellation family shares poll_once_then_drop, which polls once with a noop waker and asserts Pending. That is racy by construction — it assumes the first poll cannot complete the work. Fixing it to poll to a real yield point would retire bn-31 and this whole family of intermittent failures. Same class as bn-2cye (seed_profile asserting a microsecond-scale ordering). Both are \"test the mechanism, not its timing shadow\"."}	blake3:0797x0aA3Fw9zjkpDIvf4OK-A8J6ZYvs0NlYxhEFk7c
1785377501779010	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExohE		item.move	bn-18ab	{"state":"done"}	blake3:_payB-HTeaBc20EGH4bYht7U3R297ejHbmpMDtgMGcA
1785537052760486	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoxE		item.create	bn-3c6a	{"description":"Five tests across crates/mess-store/tests/engine_publish_cancel.rs and crates/mess-store/tests/owned_append.rs share the poll_once_then_drop helper and ASSERT its result: that one poll with a noop waker leaves the append still Pending. Whether the append completes inside poll 1 is a scheduling race — under host load the whole append can resolve synchronously and the setup assertion fires. Observed failures across six full-suite runs on 2026-07-29/30: dropped_append_future_does_not_gap_the_position_sequence (trunk), cancel_then_same_stream_retry_never_double_writes_version (bn-31n original), one owned_append cancellation test. bn-31n diagnosed this but its fix was tied to the rejected publication-gate architecture and was closed without merge; this bone fixes the tests on current main. Fix shape: make in-flight-ness a retried precondition, not an asserted outcome — bounded retry of the setup (fresh append, poll once, drop) until poll reports Pending, adjusting each test's exact-count accounting (delivered totals, borrowed_batches, heads) for attempts that completed instead. The properties under test (dropped append still publishes untorn, gate held so retry conflicts, no position gap, owner slots/bytes released) must NOT be weakened.","kind":"bug","title":"flaky: poll_once_then_drop tests assert a scheduling race in their setup (engine_publish_cancel + owned_append)"}	blake3:0wuaXFPY3HH_N7y6sNhEDROLiQetGWPUlxonOQidMjo
1785537132525497	mess-poll-flake	itc:v3:AWLycnJkknJJMk0AAAAAQfXXWquqtWAAAAEzAQ		item.move	bn-3c6a	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:KWh1BvqXjn9qRRhDNMNHOQbXbXqKXVtsb0tS70NwbzU
1785537132538614	mess-poll-flake	itc:v3:AWLycnJkknJJMk0AAAAAQfXXWquqtWAAAAEzAg		item.assign	bn-3c6a	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-poll-flake"}	blake3:awPihrb6Bmp8Dl0xOR7JyumAC93PoPK5rfLiQSdDa-0
1785537157325465	mess-fsync-flake	itc:v3:AWLPJM55OSZmdAAAAAAAQd6t3rq23AAAAAEuAQ		item.move	bn-2cye	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:S5WreejAEyzrTFpb8H6fzzKNTM-eYukymHvTq125g5o
1785537157340332	mess-fsync-flake	itc:v3:AWLPJM55OSZmdAAAAAAAQd6t3rq23AAAAAEuAg		item.assign	bn-2cye	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-fsync-flake"}	blake3:zjnjgFaL_JrMVHMvcGcXXzyzVlVrhGqatUissDtygRY
1785538001410094	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpBE		item.comment	bn-3c6a	{"body":"Implemented in workspace bn-3c6a (commits ae2ef113 code, 0575214b seal review record). Review cr-1fmgm9 created, reviewers=[mess-dev], NOT self-approved.\n\nWHAT CHANGED\n- Both files: poll_once_then_drop now returns Option<F::Output> (None = still Pending = really dropped in flight; Some(out) = the append simply finished inside poll 1 and nothing was cancelled). New local bounded wrapper drop_in_flight(mk_future) -> attempts retries fresh-append/poll-once/drop until an attempt is provably Pending, panics on a completed-with-Err attempt, and hard-fails after MAX_DROP_ATTEMPTS=50 with a message saying the hot path stopped parking (real behaviour change, not host-load noise). Helpers stay local per file; module/helper doc prose rewritten to describe the retry contract.\n- engine_publish_cancel::dropped_append_future_does_not_gap_the_position_sequence: per-stream attempt vector; attempt k appends at At(2*completed); delivered total = CANCELS + 2*sum(attempts) + FOLLOWERS + 1; final head per stream = At(2*attempts_on_that_stream). assert_consistent unchanged.\n- engine_publish_cancel::cancel_then_same_stream_retry_never_double_writes_version: attempt k appends at At(completed); retry is issued at before_drop=At(attempts-1) and must Conflict with actual=after_drop=At(attempts); stream page length = attempts+1 with dense positions asserted in a loop. No hardcoded At(0)/At(1) left.\n- owned_append::cancellation_after_owned_transfer_still_publishes and ::barriered_fallback_survives_cancellation_after_submission: attempt k at At(2*completed); expected read length 1+2*attempts with the [1;32]/[2;32] pattern checked for EVERY attempt; borrowed_batches = 1+attempts, borrowed_records = 1+2*attempts; owner_intent slots/bytes still asserted 0.\n- owned_append::cancelled_new_name_owned_unit_publishes_reopens_and_conflicts: each attempt uses a FRESH stream name (fresh-owned-stream-{n}) because a completed attempt permanently interns the name, so every attempt is still a genuine new-name unit; assertions target the dropped attempt's stream, and the reopen loop now checks every attempt's stream recovered exactly one record; the stale-retry Conflict (NoStream vs At(0)) is unchanged.\n\nTEST EVIDENCE (all in the workspace, CLANG_PATH=/usr/bin/clang)\n1. cargo nextest run -p mess-store --test engine_publish_cancel --test owned_append -> 18/18 passed.\n2. Stability loop: 20 iterations of that same command -> 20/20 passed, 0 failed (360 test executions).\n3. Load stress on the built binaries (no cargo lock contention), 3 rounds x 24 concurrent copies of each binary at --test-threads 8, host load average peaked 24.00 -> 144/144 process exits 0, zero failures. With temporary instrumentation the retry path fired NATURALLY 11 times (10x one retry, 1x two retries) in that run: the race is real on this host and the retry absorbs it.\n4. Forced-retry correctness: temporary MESS_FORCE_COMPLETED_ATTEMPTS hook that drives the first N attempts to full completion -> all 5 tests pass with N=1,2,3 (verified the forced completions actually fired via stdout markers).\n5. Mutation check that the new counts are still EXACT: reverting cancel_events to the hardcoded CANCELS*2 and borrowed_records to 3 makes the tests fail under N=1 (left 71 vs right 59; left 5 vs right 3). Restored afterwards.\n6. just fmt-check clean; cargo clippy on both test targets produces no warnings in these files.\nAll instrumentation was removed before committing (verified by grep and a final clean 18/18 run).\n\nFull `just test` intentionally NOT run per lead instruction (lead runs it once before merge). Workspace left unmerged, bone state doing, claims held."}	blake3:lBS6AH7NFhs6qShE8M8KBpDEYcb8_mH2PjJEBbifUuI
1785538154991564	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpRE		item.move	bn-3c6a	{"state":"done"}	blake3:yyQSuKqg0qzFHoHPvfyQ3aI2FiqZ1A23WHbgVm28gGQ
1785538155013521	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExphE		item.comment	bn-3c6a	{"body":"Merged to trunk @ c0f20093 (review cr-1fmgm9 approved by mess-dev). All five poll_once_then_drop tests now retry the cancellation setup until an attempt is provably dropped in flight, with every exact-count assertion derived from the attempt count. Worker evidence: 20/20 stability loops; 24-way load stress 144/144 with the retry firing naturally 11x; forced-completion hook green at N=1,2,3; mutation checks confirm derived counts are load-bearing. Lead independently re-ran 18/18 + fmt-check."}	blake3:5KNKeI6vUv048RNXXuyTC3fwxnp4uf_p_TMYtZwaoxU
1785538379271947	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpxE		item.comment	bn-2cye	{"body":"Fixed via option (c) — assert the mechanism, counted, not timed.\n\nMECHANISM CHOSEN: $registry record count. Fsync count is NOT usable here.\n- fresh_prof_backend() opens LogEngine with default options = Durability::Process, which issues no log barrier at all. Measured on this host with the harness's exact construction: 64 warm appends -> commit.fsync delta 0; 64 new-stream appends -> commit.fsync delta 0. No fsync signal exists to assert on.\n- Root cause: bn-2di deleted the fjall name tables and the bn-150 SyncAll with them. A name persist is now a $registry batch written by the same committer into the same segment in the same commit group as the append that references it (see crates/mess-store/tests/engine_name_durability.rs). That is ALSO why the p50 gap shrank to ~1us and started landing on the wrong side — there is no multi-ms flush left to make the ordering safe.\n- $registry records, by contrast, are exact: 64 new-stream commands -> 65 records (64 stream names + 1 'rel-touch' event-type name); 256 warm appends to an already-interned stream -> 0 (REG4: one record per name ever, never one per event). Asserted with assert_eq!.\n\nCHANGE: examples/social/tests/seed_profile.rs only. No production code touched — the probe reaches the engine through already-public API (ProfilingBackend::inner() is a new accessor on the harness's own wrapper; FjallSnapshotBackend::inner(), LogEngine::read_stream/total_events were already pub) and bypasses the ProfilingBackend so it never pollutes the histograms it is compared against. run_new_streams_seq/run_warm_one_stream now also return the $registry delta over their measured window; the ignored matrix prints it as a 'registry name persists' row and its stale 'durability flush' wording is corrected. The append_batch-observed and load_snapshot-called assertions are kept. Module + test doc comments rewritten to state the counted mechanism.\n\nEVIDENCE (workspace bn-2cye, commit b2aeb4f7):\n- cargo nextest run -p social --test seed_profile: 10/10 pass in a loop; +1 pass after rebase onto epoch c0f20093 (11/11 total).\n- cargo nextest run -p social (122 tests, parallel): 5/5 pass.\n- cargo clippy -p social --tests: no new warnings.\n- just fmt-check: clean.\n- Not run: full 'just test' (lead runs it pre-merge). A 3x 'social + mess-store' parallel-load run was attempted but mess-store's snapshot_plus_tail_equals_full_replay (3000 iters) exceeded the 10m tool timeout; aborted cleanly, no stray processes. The new assertion has no timing component, so load cannot affect it.\n\nReview: cr-22tcuq (not self-approved). Workspace bn-2cye left active, bone left in 'doing', claims held."}	blake3:IwLVBjKf2V-HH46n9yhxwtEQLKqMTgZywri6fI81atk
1785538482930829	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqBE		item.move	bn-2cye	{"state":"done"}	blake3:8PA2mOfp5BVMc8AQEHkMkw0-jkpvcPy3r7XmTDUVABA
1785538482959221	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqRE		item.comment	bn-2cye	{"body":"Merged to trunk (review cr-22tcuq approved by mess-dev). Fix is option (c) as planned, but the mechanism turned out to be the $registry record count, not fsyncs: under Durability::Process the harness issues no barrier at all, and bn-2di already removed the per-name SyncAll (bn-150) — which is the real reason the p50 ordering lost its margin and flaked. Smoke now asserts new_reg == 64+1 exactly and warm_reg == 0 exactly. Zero production changes; probe bypasses the ProfilingBackend."}	blake3:S8YE9i7-a0Ou_NG4oyAf2JlUsQfCRZ6IHFG1Y0mIJYk
1785540431707805	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqhE		item.create	bn-3hm4	{"description":"Spike F's microblock numbers (2.2-5.3x apply, faster head/tail, lower memory) are COMPONENT-level. The end-to-end win available to Phase 6 is bounded by the fraction of composed-engine time actually spent in the stream-side ActiveIndex (crates/mess-index/src/active.rs): if apply+lookup+iteration is X% of engine time under the reference workloads, even the best-case 5.3x component win moves end-to-end throughput by less than X*(1-1/5.3) — Amdahl. Dense heads (bn-34v8/bn-2ig9) died on exactly this: head(name) vs stream_id_of(name) landed within 1%, proving the cost was lock acquisition, not the work a fancier structure would speed up; bn-18ab then captured the real win with a one-line primitive change. Task: attribute composed-engine CPU time to mess_index::active operations under mess-bench reference workloads (perf profiling preferred, temporary counter instrumentation in a scratch workspace as fallback), per workload. Also estimate ActiveIndex resident memory if cheaply available. Deliverable: a bone comment with the per-workload attribution table and a keep/drop recommendation for the bn-1fsu port. If ActiveIndex-attributable time is under ~5% everywhere, Phase 6's replacement cannot pay as a perf hypothesis and the phase should be declined or rescoped; if it is a double-digit share somewhere, bn-1fsu proceeds with that workload as its target metric.","kind":"task","title":"Phase 6 premise check: measure ActiveIndex share of composed-engine time before porting microblocks"}	blake3:qnQIPRP5SLiQJgxLR7TihIBIKR-lZkQZUHoOeo8xrow
1785540443366768	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqxE		item.link	bn-1fsu	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3hm4"}	blake3:-sLAVlsHXWjwvu0_y7Hq_I8SCDfGO0381yqMcVvSIj8
1785540443394762	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrBE		item.comment	bn-34as	{"body":"Hypothesis-first reorder (same treatment as bn-34v8/dense heads): bn-3hm4 now blocks bn-1fsu. Spike F's 2.2-5.3x is component-level; the composed-engine win is bounded by ActiveIndex's actual share of engine time under the reference workloads. Measure that share first — if it's a few percent, the four size-m risk-high bones ahead of the gate cannot pay and the phase gets declined/rescoped on evidence; if it's double-digit somewhere, bn-1fsu proceeds with a concrete target metric."}	blake3:1m4RvqE_zq27NPk6tQraOeA0z7DEinllCa3m5Hz71Lk
1785540503406629	mess-premise-p6	itc:v3:AWKck5PkySTyTJQAAAAAQbq6-tVerUAAAAEyAQ		item.move	bn-3hm4	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:PMa2rmlyXd5rVMwe0YZdahWRfvIVQaALunEA_VIQWAc
1785540503412173	mess-premise-p6	itc:v3:AWKck5PkySTyTJQAAAAAQbq6-tVerUAAAAEyAg		item.assign	bn-3hm4	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-premise-p6"}	blake3:qsgaYrDGk3ABIEs0K98ntXans2IsRQwZr0UfrDU8kLQ
1785541554786007	mess-premise-p6	itc:v3:AWKck5PkySTyTJQAAAAAQbq6-tVerUAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-3hm4	{"body":"MEASURED — ActiveIndex is 0.0006%-0.50% of every reference workload. RECOMMENDATION: DROP the phase as a perf hypothesis (bn-1fsu and the three bones behind it cannot pay).\n\n## Methodology\n\nTwo methods, agreeing.\n\n(1) Counter instrumentation (primary). maw workspace bn-3hm4 (epoch 4efee6be, from main). A `mess_index::probe` module with process-global atomics wraps EVERY ActiveIndex entry point (apply_committed, stream_head, resolve, stream_entries, stream_entries_from, global_range, global_committed, snapshot; applied_end is count-only — its body is one atomic load). mess-bench got a temporary `--only <workload>` filter and reset/report pairs around each MEASURED window (not the whole workload function — corpus building is excluded). Build: plain `cargo build --release`. Runs: 2 full `run --mode full` passes + 5 targeted `--only` passes.\n\n(2) perf (cross-check). `CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=true RUSTFLAGS=\"-C force-frame-pointers=yes\"`, `perf record -g --call-graph fp -F 20000`, attribution by inclusive stack match on `mess_index::active::ActiveIndex>::`. perf_event_paranoid=2 on this host, so samples are USER-SPACE ONLY — the denominator excludes kernel/syscall/idle time, which INFLATES ActiveIndex's apparent share. That bias favours the phase, and the phase still loses.\n\nProbe-overhead control: ABBA (on/off/off/on) with `MESS_PROBE_OFF` at the worst case (200,001 apply calls in one window). on = 1.5730 s / 1.5889 s; off = 1.5667 s / 1.6279 s. No measurable probe distortion — the timing probes are inside run-to-run noise.\n\nHost caveat: another agent's TLC/Apalache JVMs pushed loadavg to 5-8 during the batch-shape sweep (noted per cell below); the reference-suite runs were at loadavg 2.5-3.9, which is this box's desktop baseline. Contamination raised the measured share by ~25% in the one cell measured both ways (0.072-0.081% quiet vs 0.091-0.097% loaded) — wrong direction to change any conclusion.\n\n## Per-workload attribution (share of the MEASURED window)\n\nSix of the nine reference workloads make ZERO ActiveIndex calls. They do not go through the composed engine at all:\n\n  buffered append (mess_log)      0 calls   0.000%\n  durable append (mess_log)       0 calls   0.000%\n  sealed pointer replay           0 calls   0.000%\n  sealed payload codec            0 calls   0.000%\n  fold-chain overhead             0 calls   0.000%\n  load_verified throughput        0 calls   0.000%\n  recovery time                   0 calls   0.000%   <- uses mess_log::recover_all, never mess_index\n\nThe three that touch it (run1 / run2):\n\n  workload window                  calls  ActiveIndex time    share      apply/read\n  engine.buffered.append           401    0.056 / 0.063 ms    0.072% / 0.081%   100% apply\n  engine.sealed_replay             1      0.0020 / 0.0020 ms  0.0006%           100% read\n  reader_contention.writer_solo    400    0.173 / 0.096 ms    0.496% / 0.286%   100% apply\n  reader_contention.writer_loaded  400    0.180 / 0.116 ms    0.416% / 0.331%   100% apply\n\nMAX across the whole reference suite: 0.50%.\n\nRead-path split: essentially nil. The only read-path call anywhere in the suite is ONE `stream_entries` in the sealed-replay window (2 us on a 327 ms window). `stream_head`, `resolve`, `global_range`, `global_committed` and `snapshot` were called ZERO times in every run. reader_contention's readers use `Backend::head` / `stream_id_of`, which read `book.heads` and `book.registry` under the Book RwLock — they never touch the ActiveIndex. `ActiveIndex::resolve` has no caller in mess-store at all (only `mess_index::sealed::store::resolve_with`, which mess-store does not call).\n\nperf cross-check (inclusive share of USER CPU samples, whole workload):\n\n  engine:             ActiveIndex 11 / 26,098 = 0.042%   [seal_active 56.3% | zstd 49.2% | tokio 32.8% | materialize 15.7% | append_batch subtree 5.7% | crc32c 3.9%]\n  reader_contention:  ActiveIndex 13 / 10,448 = 0.124%   [tokio 88.5% | append_batch subtree 13.6% | stream_id_of 6.7% | crc32c 6.4% | Book RwLock 3.4% | publish_batch 0.30%]\n\nBoth methods land in the same place. Note reader_contention: the WHOLE `publish_batch` step — apply_committed plus the `book.heads` write-lock plus the watermark publish — is 0.30% of user CPU. Deleting it entirely would buy 0.3%.\n\n## Why: batching has already amortised apply away\n\n`publish_batch` (engine.rs:1067) calls `apply_committed` ONCE PER BATCH with a single-element slice. Production entries are per-BATCH, not per-event (D-FMT-6 stream-constant batches). The reference workloads append 2,000-5,000 events per batch, so 2,000,002 events cost 401 apply calls. Measured per-event cost: 0.028-0.032 ns/event (engine) and 0.12-0.22 ns/event (reader_contention), against a total append cost of ~39 ns/event. Spike F's 2.2-5.3x is a per-APPLY win; production does ~1/2000th as many applies as Spike F's shape implies.\n\nBatch-shape sensitivity (engine append window; two reps each, order reversed between sweeps):\n\n  shape             apply calls   window          apply share\n  400 x 5,000       401           0.099-0.102 s   0.091% / 0.097%\n  20,000 x 100      20,001        0.364-0.400 s   0.565% / 0.569%\n  200,000 x 1       200,001       1.57-1.86 s     0.907% / 1.008%  (+ 1.004% / 1.016% in the ABBA control)\n\nEven at Spike F's most favourable one-event-per-batch shape — a shape production's own committer exists to avoid — apply is ~1% of the append window.\n\n(continued in the next comment: Amdahl bound, memory, engine reopen, recommendation, caveats.)"}	blake3:xFpezfwPcU8EbBV52LUy7AnK1mwLzY4AViZkM6fYCr0
1785541554805325	mess-premise-p6	itc:v3:AWKck5PkySTyTJQAAAAAQbq6-tVerUAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-3hm4	{"body":"(bn-3hm4 premise check, part 2 of 2 — continues the previous comment.)\n\n## Amdahl bound on the end-to-end win\n\nBest case (5.3x): gain = X * (1 - 1/5.3) = X * 0.811\nMid case  (2.2x): gain = X * (1 - 1/2.2) = X * 0.545\n\n  workload                          X        best (5.3x)   mid (2.2x)\n  engine.buffered.ev_per_s          0.081%   +0.066%       +0.044%\n  engine.sealed_replay.ev_per_s     0.0006%  +0.0005%      +0.0003%\n  reader_contention writer_solo     0.496%   +0.402%       +0.270%\n  reader_contention writer_loaded   0.416%   +0.337%       +0.227%\n  every other reference workload    0%       0%            0%\n  (one-event-batch shape, N/A)      1.008%   +0.818%       +0.550%\n\nThe gate that would judge this is mess-bench's -10% per-metric ratchet, and observed run-to-run drift on these very metrics is percent-scale (writer_retention read 0.81 here vs bn-18ab's 0.95/0.98 post-RwLock on the same code). The entire predicted effect is one to two orders of magnitude below the noise floor of the instrument that would have to detect it. Phase 6 cannot produce a measurable end-to-end result on the reference suite by construction.\n\n## Memory\n\nLive ActiveIndex resident estimate (StreamEntry 40 B + GlobalEntry 48 B per BATCH, plus HashMap slots and Vec slack):\n\n  engine, 400 x 5,000 (2,000,002 events, 2 streams)   401 entries      44,902 B   = 0.022 B/event\n  reader_contention (1,600,129 events, 65 streams)    928 entries      94,103 B   = 0.059 B/event\n  engine, 200,000 x 1 (200,002 events, 2 streams)     200,001 entries  20,975,462 B = 104.9 B/event\n\nAt the reference batch shape the whole hot index is 45 KB. Spike F's \"lower memory\" claim is real but is a claim about a structure that occupies tens of kilobytes here. (The module doc's \"~1M entries per segment\" assumption only holds for near-unbatched writes.)\n\n## The one place the number is double-digit — and it still does not pay\n\nEngine reopen (`LogEngine::open_with`, engine.rs:3178) applies every hot entry in ONE bulk apply_committed. This is NOT in the reference suite (the `recovery` workload measures mess_log::recover_all, not engine reopen), so I measured it separately, two reps each:\n\n  shape             hot entries   reopen window   apply share\n  400 x 5,000       401           33.9 / 35.5 ms  0.040% / 0.041%\n  200,000 x 1       200,001       30.8 / 31.8 ms  18.6% / 19.3%\n\n19% is a real double-digit share. But: it requires an unsealed log of 200k single-event batches, the absolute saving at 5.3x is ~4.8 ms (2.2x: ~3.2 ms) on a one-shot cold-start path, and at the reference batch shape it collapses to 0.04%. That is not four size-m risk:high bones (bn-1fsu -> bn-1yi7/bn-dxv0 -> the Loom/reclamation work), it is at most a future micro-bone if engine reopen latency ever becomes a complaint.\n\n## Recommendation: DROP\n\nDecline Phase 6's stream-side ActiveIndex replacement as a performance hypothesis. bn-1fsu has no target metric it can move: there is no reference workload where ActiveIndex is above 0.5%, and the best available component win converts that to at most +0.40% end-to-end — unmeasurable against a -10% ratchet and percent-scale drift.\n\nThis is the bn-34v8/bn-2ig9 pattern verbatim. There, `head(name)` vs `stream_id_of(name)` landed within 1%, proving the cost was lock acquisition rather than the head-map read a fancier structure would have sped up. Here the same shape appears one level out: in reader_contention, tokio scheduling is 88.5% of user CPU, name resolution + Book RwLock is ~10%, and the entire index apply is 0.12%. The time is around the ActiveIndex, not in it, and batching already removed the per-apply cost microblocks exist to reduce.\n\nIf the phase is kept alive at all, RESCOPE it to: (a) delete `ActiveIndex::resolve` or find it a caller — it is currently unreachable from mess-store; (b) a measurement-first micro-bone on engine-reopen rebuild cost with many small batches, gated on someone actually complaining about reopen latency. Neither justifies bn-1fsu as written.\n\n## Caveats / not covered\n\n- The reference suite has no live-tail or global-position read workload, so `global_range` / `global_committed` are entirely unmeasured (0 calls). If a `read_global_page` workload is ever added, re-check — that is the one read path with a non-trivial body (a Vec clone of the committed prefix).\n- No multi-stream paged `read_stream` workload either; `stream_entries_from` was exercised once, on a single stream.\n- perf's denominator is user-space CPU only (paranoid=2), which overstates ActiveIndex's share; the counters' denominator is wall time of the measured window. They differ by ~2-5x on tiny sample counts (11 and 13 samples) and agree on the order of magnitude.\n- Absolute throughputs in these runs: engine.buffered 25.9 M ev/s, reader_contention writer_solo 23.0 M / loaded 18.7 M, retention 0.81. The only floor failure was phase5.foldchain.full_chain (1.69 M vs 1.75 M floor), a BLAKE3 CPU workload unrelated to this bone and plausibly the background JVM load. writer_retention 0.81 vs bn-18ab's post-RwLock 0.95/0.98 is worth a separate look by someone — not this bone.\n\nArtifacts (not in trunk): workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-3hm4 (instrumentation, NEVER MERGE — for the lead to destroy); logs/ledgers/perf data under /tmp/claude-1000/-home-bob-src-mess/768f17cd-2aee-447c-8b0f-28effa2be6fc/scratchpad/ (run1.log, run2.log, ledger1.json, ledger2.json, perf-engine.data, perf-rc.data); profiling build at /home/bob/.cache/mess-perf-target/."}	blake3:I4Sv6fC5r5JrMWKUJ8aMliYvi6Ss76nanJjQTkdi8OI
1785542023496626	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrRE		item.create	bn-40yp	{"description":"The floor (1,949,088) was seeded from docs/perf/envelope.md's 2026-07-09 measurement, taken via a DIFFERENT harness (the ignored fold_chain_overhead cargo test, quiet host) and carrying bn-1l6's own caveat that such rows read 5-13% optimistic. mess-bench on current trunk measures 1.6966-1.7057M across 4 runs (2 lead, 2 premise-check worker) under ambient desktop load 1.9-3.9 — a <1% cluster sitting -12.8% off the seed, tripping the -10% tolerance on every run. Not a code regression: fold_chain.rs is untouched since a fmt pass, the workload is pure BLAKE3 (no engine/lock code), blake3 pinned 1.8.5 since Cargo.lock exists, and bn-18ab's validation passed this floor 2 days ago on identical code — the variable is host conditions. Re-seed floor to 1,700,000 (alarm at 1.53M) so the ratchet measures the code, not the desktop.","kind":"task","title":"Re-seed phase5.foldchain.full_chain floor from current in-suite measurements"}	blake3:ry--UkNNeq1xA6t56wpE26GpUS1VtVOqnNih3zIjIhA
